<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Headless UI smoke tests (#741), kept in their own project so the fast logic suite stays fast and
    free of an Avalonia application lifetime. These render real views into a headless surface, which is
    the only place a reflection binding or a missing resource actually fails - the app disables compiled
    bindings globally, so those errors exist only at runtime.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>OpenTabletArtist.UiTests</RootNamespace>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Microsoft Testing Platform, not VSTest (#756). xunit v3 test projects are executables that
         host their own runner, and OutputType=Exe is what xunit v3 requires once Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk
         is no longer there to set it. Which runner `dotnet test` uses is named once for the repo, in
         global.json, rather than per project:

             "test": { "runner": "Microsoft.Testing.Platform" }

         So xunit.runner.visualstudio, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk and coverlet.collector are gone: the
         first two are the VSTest path, and coverlet.collector is a VSTest data collector that nothing
         collected — no workflow ran with coverage and there is no .runsettings in the tree.

         The global.json route rather than the per-project TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport property,
         because the two differ in a way that matters (#939). Under the property, `dotnet test` accepts
         a VSTest option such as a filter, warns MTP0001 that it is being ignored, runs the WHOLE suite
         and exits 0 — so narrowing to one test gives a green result for a run nobody asked for, and a
         script requesting coverage or a logger gets the same false success. Under the runner named
         here the same option is refused outright: "Unknown option", exit 5, and no tests run.

         Filtering then uses the platform's own options (filter-class, filter-method, filter-namespace)
         through `dotnet test`. docs/dev/BUILDING.md has the commands and what IDE discovery needs.

         CI is unaffected: it runs `dotnet test <csproj>` in its usual no-build form with no filter, a
         failing test exits 2, and a bad option exits 5 (all checked, not assumed). -->

    <!-- Avalonia.Headless.XUnit 12.x is built on xunit v3, which is why this project moved there first.
         The logic suite followed (#756), so all three suites now sit on one major and one runner.

         This package is what pins the major: it is compiled against xunit.v3.extensibility.core 3.2.2,
         and 4.x changed TestIntrospectionHelper.GetTestCaseDetails. Its NuGet dependency is a floor,
         not a pin, so restore resolves 4.x happily and the build stays clean while 106 of the 110
         tests here fail at discovery with:

             MissingMethodException: Method not found:
             'ValueTuple`8<...> Xunit.v3.TestIntrospectionHelper.GetTestCaseDetails(...)'

         Measured, not predicted: the bump to 4.0.1 was tried and that is what came back. Only the four
         tests that do not use AvaloniaFact/AvaloniaTheory survive it.

         Tracked upstream at AvaloniaUI/Avalonia#22072, where a maintainer has marked it a feature
         request rather than a bug: bumping Avalonia's own dependency would break users not ready to
         move, so shims for both versions are the likely route. There is no date to wait for.
         Avalonia.Headless.XUnit 12.1.3, the newest published, still declares 3.2.2.

         The runner is already named in global.json, so nothing about the opt-in has to change when it
         does land: what blocked the upgrade was only ever this package's compiled dependency.

         #935 no longer waits on that. The flake there was the VSTest adapter rejecting the assembly-
         info JSON after 3.2.2's in-process runner appended a shutdown message to it (xunit/xunit#3576,
         fixed upstream in 4.0.0) - and that adapter is no longer in the path at all, so the failure
         mode is gone rather than mitigated. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Headless.XUnit" Version="12.1.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Shared with the logic suite rather than copied: one fake daemon, so a change to
         IDaemonTransport can't leave two drifting stand-ins behind. -->
    <!-- The library suite owns these now; both app suites compile them from there so there is one copy. -->
    <Compile Include="..\OtdInterop.Tests\MemorySettingsFileStore.cs" Link="MemorySettingsFileStore.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\OtdInterop.Tests\FakeDaemonTransport.cs" Link="FakeDaemonTransport.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\OtdInterop.Tests\NullOtdLog.cs" Link="NullOtdLog.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\OpenTabletArtist\OpenTabletArtist.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
